findings.body:
Removing `escapeHtml` and feeding `body` straight into `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` lets any HTML (including `<script>`, `onclick`, or `<img onerror>`) execute. If `body` ever contains attacker-controlled text the page is now vulnerable to XSS.

```suggestion
// TODO: implement a safe rich-text renderer (e.g. marked + DOMPurify or a constrained allow-list) instead of trusting raw HTML.
export function Comment({ body }: { body: string }) {
  return <div className="comment" dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: sanitize(body) }} />;
}
```
